E={8: 'Lights shoot across the cave, lighting the underground with colors of every kind, warm and cold, swirling overhead. The countless bits of saturated lights reveal the black, white, and red of a circus tent drooping around every side of her, far above her head, with walls as far out as a long walk. The figure’s shoe that was previously touched by her is now darting to the edge of the tent cover, running away in its black, white, and red scheme. The lights dance on and off, not in strobes, but in fast breaths. Their warmth washes over her face, then leaves it cold. There is a crowd of puppets smiling from tiers of benches at another side of the circus tent, their smiles fixed and their porcelain skin reflecting brightly. Their lacquered cheeks shine, and their glass eyes follow her, unblinking. These little toys of boys and girls are adorned in the classical formal attire of the early twentieth century, all looking like children. The smell of paint and old varnish drifts from them.', 40: 'Alice is darting her eyes back and forth again, now standing in disbelief. She is clothed in a dark-black dress with yellow chaos stars of many points decorating the exterior. She walks around barefoot across the ground, thinking of where to exit. The dirt is cool and gritty under her soles. Her tears are no longer flowing, but her face is still red and wet.', 48: '“I think it is fitting we bring out the beast!” the Jester says aloud with foot swings, after the hook swings around to face the audience.', 52: '“Come on, dummies! Pelt her!” the Jester says to the crowd of spectators. Circus music is still playing in roundabouts. The dolls begin to chant in small voices, “Pelt! Pelt! Pelt!”', 56: '“Throw the tomatoes!” the Jester shrieks in his high-pitched scream, spit spewing from his tiny ram-like face wrapped in bulging veins.', 58: 'The tomatoes do fly at her, or so she thinks them to be tomatoes… She flinches at the sight of one cranking his arm back for a pitch. The first one strikes heavier than imagined, splashing hard against her arm, now red. She looks at the ground, and she sees it pumping—a beating red heart. Blood covers her arm. It is warm and wet, and it smells of iron. More hearts fly, some missing and some landing. They slap against her skin, squelch in the dirt, and keep beating where they lie. The Jester—Fool lets out a wicked laughter, a squeal like a demon. The lights begin turning down, as the music plays louder, making it impossible for anyone to hear her words.', 62: 'The bull is looking dead on at Alice in his four-legged, jacked sprint, his path throwing dust clouds as his exhales fume with loud wet anger. Hooves hammer the ground faster and faster, until the thunder runs up through the dirt and into her knees before she hears it. Spit and froth fly from his jaw, and the heat of his breath reaches her before he does. “Murder her!” the Jester screams, and the dolls gasp in a single breath. Alice, now terrified, like a deer in the headlights, locks up, and it seems almost like a dream to her. In one moment, the bull—goat slams hard into her, its horns gnashing into her skull and teeth hard, throwing her head backwards into a crashing drop of skull, spilling more red, but now from her brains. The crunch is wet and loud, and her body is lifted and thrown, then trampled over, almost stumbling the stride pace into a tripping, but the beast catches itself. Red squirts fire out like wet balloons popped from her torso being stomped apart, her head taking the full force of a right leg propelled through her jaw. The sound of an old record skips with skirling laughter, loudly. The Jester smiles with even greater perversion than before, pleased at the destruction of Alice.'}
